# NOTES
- Structural finding (Czech/Slovak/Hungarian/Bulgarian VVER RPVs): the current dataset's "Izhora Plants" value is copy-pasted identically across all 15 Czech/Slovak units plus all 11 Hungarian/Bulgarian units (26 units total) with zero supporting citation for any of them. Cross-checking against Wikipedia (EN/CS/SK/HU/BG), WNA, and Skoda JS's own company history found NOT ONE source naming Izhora for any Czech or Slovak plant. Best-supported correction: Dukovany (1-4), Temelin (1-2), Bohunice V-213 units (3-4), Bohunice A-1, and Mochovce (1-4) were built with domestically-manufactured Skoda (Skoda JS Plzen) vessels — directly documented for Temelin with a dated citation (first RPV arrived on site July 10, 1991, made by Skoda Plzen), inferred with MED confidence elsewhere via Skoda JS's own claim of having built 21 complete VVER-440 reactors and 3 VVER-1000 reactors. Bohunice V1 (units 1-2, oldest V-230 design) is the one exception left genuinely UNVERIFIABLE — WNA describes joint Atomenergoexport/Skoda supply without specifying which entity forged the vessel.
- For Hungary (Paks 1-4) and Bulgaria (Kozloduy 1-6), the same "Izhora Plants" and "Skoda" turbine values could NOT be positively confirmed OR disconfirmed this pass — Hungarian/Bulgarian Wikipedia and WNA country profiles carry no unit-level contractor detail, and IAEA PRIS (the ideal source) returned 403/404 throughout. These 40 fields are marked UNVERIFIABLE rather than corrected; a same-hypothesis correction (Izhora→domestic or Russian-turbine-works) is plausible but unproven and should not be applied without a stronger source.
- Confirmed miss: Paks unit 5 (Paks II, VVER-1200, under construction) is a brand-new 2020s Rosatom-led build, not the 1980s Soviet supply chain — the dataset's "Skoda" turbine and "Izhora Plants" RPV values are anachronistic. WNA's Hungary profile documents 2025-era fabrication at Arabelle Solutions (Belfort, France; GE/EDF turbine JV) for the turbine and AEM-Spetsstal (St Petersburg, Rosatom's AEM-Technologies group) for RPV forging.
- Likely miss on "constructor" field definition across Spain: the field appears to conflate the NSSS/reactor technology vendor with the actual physical builder. Explicit contrary evidence found for two of ten Spanish units: Jose Cabrera was built by Zachry Construction Corporation "for Westinghouse," and Vandellos 2 was built by the Spanish firm Copisa, not Westinghouse. This raises the possibility that "constructor" for the other Westinghouse-tech Spanish units (Almaraz, Asco) is also a vendor-name placeholder rather than a verified builder.
- Likely systematic miss on Spain's architect_engineer field: WNA states Spain's "second generation" of reactors (early-1970s cohort: Almaraz, Asco, Cofrentes) used local engineering firms Empresarios Agrupados and INITEC, not the foreign reactor vendor as AE — but no source found in this pass distinguishes which of the two firms applies to which specific plant, so corrected_value is left as an either/or pending a firm-specific source (Empresarios Agrupados' own site returned SSL errors; recommend a follow-up pass).
- Naming clarification (not an error): Italy's "Enrico Fermi" plant IS Trino Vercellese — same single site, both names are legitimate and refer to one plant, not two distinct reactors. No database correction needed, but worth confirming this isn't accidentally double-counted as two separate reactor records.
- Correction: Italy's Latina plant constructor/AE/RPV fields used "NPPC" (Nuclear Power Plant Company), but Wikipedia names the actual consortium as "The Nuclear Power Group" (TNPG) — a different, though similarly-named, UK reactor consortium (AEI, John Thompson, C.A. Parsons, Head Wrightson, A. Reyrolle & Co.). This is a clear entity-name substitution error, high confidence.
- Coverage caveat: WebSearch quota was exhausted early in all four research passes (session-wide 200-call caps hit before or shortly after each task began), forcing reliance on WebFetch against known/guessed URLs. IAEA PRIS — intended as a primary source — was inaccessible in every pass (403/JS-postback/paywall). This is the main reason so many turbine_supplier and several architect_engineer fields, especially for Spain, Paks 1-4, and Kozloduy, are UNVERIFIABLE rather than resolved. A follow-up pass with restored search budget and working PRIS access would likely close a meaningful share of these gaps.
